stimulus.description:
```java
class Rec {
    /** Returns base raised to the power exp, where exp is a non-negative integer. */
    public static int power(int base, int exp) {
        /* missing code */
    }
}
```

options.text:
```
int result = 1;
        for (int i = 0; i < exp; i++) {
            result *= base;
        }
        return result;
```

```
int result = 1;
        for (int i = 0; i <= exp; i++) {
            result *= base;
        }
        return result;
```

```
int result = 1;
        for (int i = 0; i < exp; i++) {
            result += base;
        }
        return result;
```

```
int result = 1;
        for (int i = 1; i < exp; i++) {
            result *= base;
        }
        return result;
```

payload.explanation_en:
The correct answer is ```
int result = 1;
        for (int i = 0; i < exp; i++) {
            result *= base;
        }
        return result;
```. The loop multiplies result by base exactly exp times, starting from 1, so power returns base raised to the power exp (and 1 when exp is 0).
```
int result = 1;
        for (int i = 0; i <= exp; i++) {
            result *= base;
        }
        return result;
``` is incorrect: Loops one time too many, so power(base, exp) returns base raised to the power exp + 1.
```
int result = 1;
        for (int i = 0; i < exp; i++) {
            result += base;
        }
        return result;
``` is incorrect: Adds base instead of multiplying by it.
```
int result = 1;
        for (int i = 1; i < exp; i++) {
            result *= base;
        }
        return result;
``` is incorrect: Starts the counter at 1, so the loop multiplies only exp - 1 times.

